[[17592186125197 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\cos x\\cos y = \\sin (xy)$. Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(0, \\frac{π}{2})$."] [17592186125197 :question/answer-fields 17592186123752] [17592186175801 :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda) = (\\lambda-4)^6(\\lambda-9)^8$, the rank of $(A-9I)^3$ equals $9$, and the rank of $(A-9I)^4$ equals $7$. If a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 9$?"] [17592186175801 :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 9\n$$\n\na generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\n$$\n(A - 9I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0} \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad (A - 9I)^3 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be a $14 \\times 14$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-9I)^3$ equals $9$, then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n$\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^3 = 14-9 = 5$.\nSo, the solutions to $(A - 9I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3$ of dimension $5$ in $\\mathbb{R}^{14}$.\n- Since the rank of $(A-9I)^4$ equals $7$, then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n$\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^4 = 14-7 = 7$.\nSo, the solutions to $(A - 9I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$ of dimension $7$ in $\\mathbb{R}^{14}$.\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3$ is a subspace of dimension $5$ in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$, which is a subspace of dimension $7$, our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$ but not in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3$.\n\nTherefore, there will be\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^4 - \\text{nullity}(A-9I)^3 = 7-5=2\n$$\n\ngeneralized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=9$."] [17592186175841 :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda) = (1+\\lambda) (4-\\lambda)^3(\\lambda-1)^5$, and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1$, there are exactly $3$ eigenvectors. If a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{9}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1$?"] [17592186175841 :question/worked-solution "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ equals the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda$.\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = (1+\\lambda) (4-\\lambda)^3(\\lambda-1)^5\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda = 1$ has multiplicity $5$. So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1$ is $5$.\n\nHowever, there are only $3$ eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 1\n$$\n\nso the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1$ is $3$.\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 1$ is $5-3=2$."] [17592186175850 :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda) = (3+\\lambda)^3(\\lambda-\\pi)^8(\\lambda-2)^3$, the nullity of $(A-\\pi I)^3$ equals $4$, and the nullity of $(A-\\pi I)^4$ equals $6$. If a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = \\pi$?"] [17592186175850 :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda = \\pi\n$$\n\na generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\n$$\n(A - \\pi I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0} \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad (A - \\pi I)^3 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^3$ has dimension $4$. This means that the solutions to $(A - \\pi I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace of dimension $4$.\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^4$ has dimension $6$. This means that the solutions to $(A - \\pi I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace of dimension $6$.\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^3$ is a subspace of dimension $4$ in $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^4$, which is a subspace of dimension $6$, our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^4$ but not in $\\text{Null}(A-\\pi I)^3$.\n\nTherefore, there will be\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-\\pi I)^4 - \\text{nullity}(A-\\pi I)^3 = 6-4=2\n$$\n\ngeneralized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda=\\pi\n$$"] [17592186176257 :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(λ) = λ^{2}(λ + 1)^{16}(λ - 8)^{2}$, the nullity of $(A + I)^{4}$ equals $4$, and the nullity of $(A + I)^{5}$ equals $7$. If a basis of $R^{20}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ =-1$?"] [17592186176257 :question/answer-fields 17592186173770] [17592186176258 :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $B$ has characteristic polynomial $p(λ) = (λ + \\sqrt{2})^{3}(λ - 2)^{21}$, the rank of $(B - 2I)^{6}$ equals $4$, and the rank of $(B - 2I)^{5}$ equals $7$. If a basis of $R^{24}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $B$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $6$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 2$?"] [17592186176258 :question/answer-fields 17592186173776] [17592186114963 :question/problem "A supply container of mass $32\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is launched downward with an initial velocity of $7\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ and continues to fall vertically. Let $v(t)$ be the container's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\geq 0$ seconds after launch, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the container is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the container’s velocity is $8\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $7.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$. Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$, and the only forces acting on the container are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t)$, expressing your answer in terms of $\\bf t$ **only.**"] [17592186114963 :question/worked-solution "First, note that only two forces act on the body: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw = mg = 32\\cdot9.8 = 313.6\\,\\textrm{N}\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D$, which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n$D=-kv$,\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF = w + D = 313.6 - kv\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= ma \\\\\n313.6 - kv &= 32\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n\\frac{dv}{dt} + \\frac{k}{32}v &= 9.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the body’s velocity is $8\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $7.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$. Substituting these values into the ODE, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n7.8 + \\dfrac{k}{32}\\cdot 8 = 9.8 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k = 8\\,\\textrm{kg/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac14v = 9.8\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $v_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac14v_c = 0$.Solving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution$v_c(t) = Ae^{-t/4}$,where $A$ is a constant of integration.\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0$. So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0$, i.e.,$v_p(t) = \\alpha$.To find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute $v_p=\\alpha$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v_p}{\\mathrm{d}t} = (\\alpha)' = 0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:$0 + \\dfrac14\\alpha = 9.8 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\alpha = \\dfrac{196}{5}$Therefore, the particular solution is $v_p(t) = \\dfrac{196}{5}$.\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nv(t) = v_c(t) + v_p(t) = Ae^{-t/4} + \\dfrac{196}{5}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the body is launched downward with an initial velocity of $7\\,\\textrm{m/s}$. So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0) = 7$, and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(0) &= Ae^{-0/4} + \\frac{196}{5} \\\\\n7 &= A + \\frac{196}{5} \\\\\nA &= -\\frac{161}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= -\\frac{161}{5}e^{-t/4} + \\frac{196}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186114964 :question/problem "A geological sampler of mass $15\\,\\textrm{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the sampler's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\geq 0$ seconds after the descent began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the sampler is proportional to its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k > 0$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the sampler’s velocity is $9\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ and instantaneous acceleration is $7.4\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.\n\nFind the constant of proportionality $k$, and the differential equation that best models the velocity. Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$, and the only forces acting on the sampler are weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n$\\quad$ The constant of proportionality is $k=$\n$(q_{0}, 1, L)$\n$(q_{0}, 1, L)$\n\n$\\quad$ The differential equation that best models the velocity is\n$(q_{0}, 2, L, m, k, lo)$"] [17592186114964 :question/worked-solution "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a{:}$\n$F = ma$\n\nIn our case, there are two forces acting on the body:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the body, which equals\n$w|= mg; = 15g$.\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D$, which is proportional to the velocity of the body (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n$D = -kv$,\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Note that, since the drag, in $N=\\text{kg}\\cdot\\text{m/s}^2$, is the product of the constant $k$ and velocity, in $\\text{m/s}$, the units of $k$ are\n$\\dfrac{\\text{N}}{\\text{m/s}} = \\dfrac{\\text{kg}\\cdot\\text{m/s}^2}{\\text{m/s}} = \\text{kg/s}$.\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF = w + D = 15g - kv\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity:\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the body, we have\n\n$$\n15g - kv = 15\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nWriting in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15\\frac{dv}{dt} + kv &= 15g \\\\\n\\frac{dv}{dt} + \\frac{k}{15}v &= g\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we're given that, at a particular moment, the body's velocity is $9\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ and instantaneous acceleration is $7.4\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.\nSo, substituting\n\n$$\nv=9, g = 9.8\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 7.4\n$$\n\ninto our model, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7.4 + \\frac{k}{15} \\cdot 9 &= 9.8 \\\\\n7.4 + \\frac{3k}{5} &= 9.8 \\\\\n\\frac{3k}{5} &= 2.4 \\\\\nk &= 4kg/s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation that models the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac4{15}v = g\n$$"] [17592186114965 :question/problem "A cryogenic sample container of mass $31\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is launched vertically downward with an initial velocity of $2.9\\,\\textrm{m/s}$. Let $v(t)$ be the container's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\geq 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance acting on the container is negligible, and the only other force acting on it is its weight due to gravity.\n\nWhat is the velocity of the container after $7$ seconds? Assume that acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$."] [17592186114965 :question/worked-solution "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a{:}$\n$F = ma$\n\nSince air resistance is negligible, the only force acting on the body is its weight due to gravity. The weight $w$ of the body is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= w \\\\\n&= mg \\\\\n&= 31g\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity:\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the container, we get\n\n$$\n31g = 31\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dv}{dt} &= g\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate to solve for $v(t)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{dv}{dt}dt &= g\\intdt \\\\\nv(t) &= gt + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\nWe're told that the initial velocity of the container is $2.9\\,\\textrm{m/s}$. So, we apply the initial condition $v(0) = 2.9$, and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\nv(0) = g \\cdot 0 + C; 2.9 = 0 + C; C = 2.9\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the container $t$ seconds after release is\n\n$$\nv(t) = gt + 2.9\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity after $7$ seconds is\n\n$$\nv(7) = 9.8\\cdot7 + 2.9 = 71.5 \\,\\textrm{m/s}\n$$"] [17592186114966 :question/problem "A sensor pod of mass $20\\,\\textrm{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the pod is proportional to its velocity and acts in the opposite direction to the motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the pod’s velocity is $8\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $6.3\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity $v_\\text{term}$ of the pod? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$, and the only forces acting on the pod are the weight due to gravity and drag."] [17592186114966 :question/worked-solution "Terminal velocity is the constant speed a falling object approaches as the upward resistive force (drag) grows to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\nThat is, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma$, the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the pod: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw = mg = 20\\cdot9.8 = 196\\,\\textrm{N}\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D$, which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n$D=-kv$,\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the pod is\n\n$$\nF = w + D = 196 - kv\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= ma \\\\\n196 - kv &= 20a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the pod’s velocity is $8\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $6.3\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$. Substituting these values into the above, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n196 - 8k &= 20 \\cdot 6.3 \\\\\n196 - 8k &= 126 \\\\\n- 8k &= -70 \\\\\nk &= 8.75kg/s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the pod is\n\n$$\nF = 196 - 8.75v\n$$\n\nWhen the pod reaches terminal velocity $v_\\text{term}$, the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n196 - 8.75v_\\text{term} = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad v_\\text{term} = \\dfrac{196}{8.75} = 22.4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the pod is\n\n$$\nv_\\text{term} = 22.4\\,\\textrm{m/s}\n$$"] [17592186115789 :question/problem "A supply crate of mass $18kg$ is dropped vertically downward with an initial velocity of $2.4m/s$ and instantaneous acceleration of $8.2m/s^{2}$. Let $v(t)$ be the crate's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction. The air resistance on the crate is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nFind, rounded to one decimal place, the velocity of the supply crate after $4$ seconds.\nAssume that only gravity, $g = 9.8m/s^{2}$, and drag act on the crate.\n\nThe velocity of the supply crate after $4$ seconds is\n{{field-1}} $m/s$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186115789 :question/answer-fields 17592186113671] [17592186115791 :question/problem "A ball of mass $16kg$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the ball is proportional to its velocity and acts in the opposite direction to the motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the ball’s velocity is $16m/s$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $4.9m/s^{2}$.\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity $v_{term}$ of the body? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8m/s^{2}$, and the only forces acting on the body are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n$v_{term} =$\n{{field-1}}\n$m/s$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186115791 :question/answer-fields 17592186113673] [17592186161676 :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function $f_{X\\mid Y}(x\\, \\mid \\, 1)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above."] [17592186161676 :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function $f_{X\\mid Y}(x\\, \\mid \\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_{X\\mid Y}(x\\, \\mid \\,y) = P(X = x \\, \\mid \\, Y=y)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\nf_{X\\mid Y}(x \\, \\mid \\, 1) = P(X = x \\, \\mid \\, Y=1)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(X = x \\, \\mid \\, Y=1) = \\dfrac{P(X = x, Y=1)}{P(Y=1)} = \\dfrac{f(x,1)}{f_Y(1)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y$, and $f_Y(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y$.\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ | $f_X(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0$ | $0.3$ |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $0.5$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $x\\in \\{0,2,4\\}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 0\\begin{vmatrix}Y = 1) &= (f(0, 1))/(f_{Y}(1)) = \\frac{0.1}{0.5} = \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ P(X = 2 \\mid Y = 1)|= (f(2, 1))/(f_{Y}(1)) = \\frac{0.3}{0.5} = \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ P(X = 4\\end{vmatrix}Y = 1)|= (f(4, 1))/(f_{Y}(1)) = \\frac{0.1}{0.5} = \\frac{1}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\, \\mid \\, 1)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ |"] [17592186161677 :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\dfrac{1}{18}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{18}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{18}$ |\n\nCompute $f_{X\\mid Y}(2 \\, \\mid \\, 2)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above."] [17592186161677 :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function $f_{X\\mid Y}(x\\, \\mid \\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_{X\\mid Y}(x\\, \\mid \\,y) = P(X = x \\, \\mid \\, Y=y)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\nf_{X\\mid Y}(2 \\, \\mid \\, 2) = P(X = 2 \\, \\mid \\, Y = 2)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(X = 2 \\, \\mid \\, Y = 2) = \\dfrac{P(X = 2, Y = 2)}{P(Y = 2)} = \\dfrac{f(2,2)}{f_Y(2)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y$, and $f_Y(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y$.\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{Y}(2) &= f(1, 2) + f(2, 2) + f(3, 2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{18} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{18}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table,\n\n$$\nf(2,2) = \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\nf_Y(2) = \\dfrac{7}{18}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(2,2) = \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nin our formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{X\\mid Y}(2 \\mid 2) &= P(X = 2 \\mid Y = 2) \\\\\n&= (f(2, 2))/(f_{Y}(2)) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{1}{6}))/((\\frac{7}{18})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186161882 :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $0.5$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.3$ |\n\nCompute $P(Y=0 \\, \\mid \\, X=1)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above."] [17592186161882 :question/worked-solution "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required probability is given by\n\n$$\nP(Y = 0 \\, \\mid \\, X = 1) = \\dfrac{P(X = 1, Y = 0)}{P(X = 1)} = \\dfrac{f(1,0)}{f_X(1)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y$, and $f_X(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X$.\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{X}(1) &= f(1, 0) + f(1, 1) \\\\\n&= 0.15 + 0.3 \\\\\n&= 0.45\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that\n\n$$\nf(1,0) = 0.15\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\nf_X(1) = 0.45\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(1,0) = 0.15\n$$\n\nin our formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Y = 0 \\mid X = 1) &= (f(1, 0))/(f_{X}(1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0.15}{0.45} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186161883 :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $0.2$ | $0$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ | $0$ | $0.1$ |\n\nCompute $P(0\\lt Y \\lt 3\\, \\mid \\, X=0)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above. Round your answer to two decimal places."] [17592186161883 :question/worked-solution "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required conditional probability is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0 < Y < 3 \\mid X = 0) &= (P((0 < Y < 3), X = 0))/(P(X = 0)) \\\\\n&= (P(X = 0, (0 < Y < 3)))/(P(X = 0)) \\\\\n&= (P(X = 0, Y = 1) + P(X = 0, Y = 2))/(P(X = 0)) \\\\\n&= (f(0, 1) + f(0, 2))/(f_{X}(0))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y$, and $f_X(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X$.\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{X}(0) &= f(0, 0) + f(0, 1) + f(0, 2) + f(0, 3) \\\\\n&= 0.2 + 0 + 0.05 + 0.1 \\\\\n&= 0.35\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that\n\n$$\nf(0,1)=0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(0,2)=0.05\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0 < Y < 3 \\mid X = 0) &= (f(0, 1) + f(0, 2))/(f_{X}(0)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0 + 0.05}{0.35} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0.05}{0.35} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186162322 :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y = 0$ | $Y = 1$ | $Y = 2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 0$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X = 1$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute $f_{Y\\mid X}(1 \\mid 0)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ shown in the table above. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186162322 :question/answer-fields 17592186159104] [17592186162383 :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y = 0$ | $Y = 2$ | $Y = 4$ | $Y = 6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 1$ | $\\frac{1}{18}$ | $\\frac{5}{18}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X = 2$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{18}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n\nCompute $P(2 \\le Y \\le 6 \\mid X = 2)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ shown in the table above.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186162383 :question/answer-fields 17592186159470] [17592186115516 :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t) = \\sin ti + \\cos tj + \\tan tk$ is\n$t ∈ (- ∞, ∞), t \\ne A A A A A$.\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer."] [17592186115516 :question/answer-fields 17592186112075] [17592186176351 :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $θ = \\frac{5π}{12}$ in spherical polar coordinates?"] [17592186176351 :question/answer-fields 17592186174334] [17592186176403 :question/problem "![](images/bc/bc0e25e290235560cca0c2c0f18b8b22a8f1909e29c1d8706fff6cfc3ca8955a.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the quarter-sphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 9$ for $y \\ge 0$ and $x \\le 0$, shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as"] [17592186176403 :question/answer-fields 17592186174646] [17592186176404 :question/problem "![](images/41/41b5ff62b86d0cc7ce341a013f1815b7bb203f5b93cb52c525844f9bb93839b2.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 4y$, shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as"] [17592186176404 :question/answer-fields 17592186174652] [17592186064536 :question/problem "$⌈e⌉ =$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186064536 :question/answer-fields 17592186061396] [17592186125124 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T: R^{8} → R^{8}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$, and $T(v_{1}) = T(v_{2})$ for some distinct vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$?\n\n1. $Ker(T) = {0}$\n2. $T$ is invertible\n3. $rank(T) < 8$"] [17592186125124 :question/answer-fields 17592186123314] [17592186124424 :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y) = 9 - x^2 - y^2$ on the line segment $\\overline {AB}$, where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0, 0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle 2, 1\\rangle$, respectively."] [17592186124424 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB}$, then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$ such that\n\n$$\nf (\\mathbf b) - f (\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f (\\mathbf c) \\cdot (\\mathbf b - \\mathbf a)\n$$\n\nNotice that the function\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = 9 - x^2 - y^2\n$$\n\nis differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB}$. So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = \\langle 0, 0\\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf b = \\langle 2, 1\\rangle\n$$\n\ninto the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(2, 1) - f(0, 0) &= ∇f(c) \\cdot (⟨2, 1⟩ - ⟨0, 0⟩) \\\\\n(9 - 2^{2} - 1^{2}) - (9 - 0^{2} - 0^{2}) &= ∇f(c) \\cdot ⟨2, 1⟩ \\\\\n- 5 &= ∇f(c) \\cdot ⟨2, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\nabla f$ and evaluating at\n\n$$\n\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1, c_2\\rangle\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f &= ⟨ - 2x,-2y⟩, \\\\\n∇f(c) &= ⟨ - 2c_{1},-2c_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5 &= ∇f⟨c⟩ \\cdot ⟨2, 1⟩ \\\\\n- 5 &= ⟨ - 2c_{1},-2c_{2}⟩ \\cdot ⟨2, 1⟩ \\\\\n- 5 &= -4c_{1} - 2c_{2} \\\\\n5 &= 4c_{1} + 2c_{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}$. So, let's first find the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y - y_{a}}{y_{b} - y_{a}} &= \\frac{x - x_{a}}{x_{b} - x_{a}} \\\\\n\\frac{y - 0}{1 - 0} &= \\frac{x - 0}{2 - 0} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{x}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1,c_2\\rangle\n$$\n\nsatisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc_{2} &= \\frac{c_{1}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation\n\n$$\n4c_1 + 2c_2 = 5\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4c_{1} + 2(\\frac{c_{1}}{2}) &= 5 \\\\\n4c_{1} + c_{1} &= 5 \\\\\n5c_{1} &= 5 \\\\\nc_{1} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find\n\n$$\nc_2 = \\dfrac{c_1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is\n\n$$\n\\left(1, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)\n$$"] [17592186124425 :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z) = x^2 + y - z^2$ on the line segment $\\overline{AB}$, where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,0,1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle 2,3,0\\rangle$, respectively."] [17592186124425 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y, z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB}$, then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$ such that\n\n$$\nf (\\mathbf b) - f (\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f (\\mathbf c) \\cdot (\\mathbf b - \\mathbf a)\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nf(x, y,z) = x^2 + y - z^2\n$$\n\nis differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB}$. So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,0,1\\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf b= \\langle 2,3,0\\rangle\n$$\n\ninto the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(2, 3, 0) - f(0, 0, 1) &= ∇f(c) \\cdot (⟨2, 3, 0⟩ - ⟨0, 0, 1⟩) \\\\\n7 - (-1) &= ∇f(c) \\cdot ⟨2, 3,-1⟩ \\\\\n8 &= ∇f(c) \\cdot ⟨2, 3,-1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\nabla f$ and evaluating at\n\n$$\n\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1, c_2, c_3\\rangle\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f &= ⟨2x, 1,-2z⟩, \\\\\n∇f(c) &= ⟨2c_{1}, 1,-2c_{3}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8 &= ∇f(c) \\cdot ⟨2, 3,-1⟩ \\\\\n8 &= ⟨2c_{1}, 1,-2c_{3}⟩ \\cdot ⟨2, 3,-1⟩ \\\\\n8 &= 4c_{1} + 3 + 2c_{3} \\\\\n5 &= 4c_{1} + 2c_{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}$. So, let's first find the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x - 0}{2} &= \\frac{y - 0}{3} = (z - 1)/((-1)) \\\\\n\\frac{x}{2} &= \\frac{y}{3} = 1 - z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx = 2t, \\quad y= 3t, \\quad z = 1-t, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\mathbf c= \\langle c_1, c_2, c_3\\rangle\n$$\n\nsatisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\nc_1 = 2t, \\quad c_2 = 3t, \\quad c_3 = 1-t\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation\n\n$$\n4c_1 + 2c_3 = 5\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(2t) + 2(1 - t) &= 5 \\\\\n8t + 2 - 2t &= 5 \\\\\n6t &= 3 \\\\\nt &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of $c_1$, $c_2$, and $c_3$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc_{1} &= 2(\\frac{1}{2}) = 1, \\\\\nc_{2} &= 3(\\frac{1}{2}) = \\frac{3}{2}, \\\\\nc_{3} &= 1 - \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(1, \\dfrac{3}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\n$$"] [17592186124429 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB}$, where $\\mathbf a=\\langle 5,3\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b=\\langle 4,2\\rangle$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B$, respectively. If $f(\\mathbf a)= 5$ and $f(\\mathbf b)=6$, then according to the mean value theorem, which of the following must be true for some point $C\\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$?\n\n1. $f_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) = -1$\n2. $f_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) = 1$\n3. $f_x(\\mathbf c) +f_y(\\mathbf c) = 2$"] [17592186124429 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB}$, then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$ such that\n\n$$\nf (\\mathbf b) - f (\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f (\\mathbf c) \\cdot (\\mathbf b - \\mathbf a)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(b) - f(a) &= ∇f(c) \\cdot (b - a) \\\\\n6 - 5 &= ⟨f_{x}(c), f_{y}(c)⟩ \\cdot (⟨4, 2⟩ - ⟨5, 3⟩) \\\\\n1 &= ⟨f_{x}(c), f_{y}(c)⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 1,-1⟩ \\\\\n1 &= -f_{x}(c) - f_{y}(c)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\nf_x(\\mathbf c) + f_y(\\mathbf c) =-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186124766 :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x, y) = 2x^{3} - y$ on the line segment $AB^(―)$, where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a = ⟨0, 1⟩$ and $b = ⟨2, 2⟩$, respectively."] [17592186124766 :question/answer-fields 17592186121163] [17592186129596 :question/problem "Consider the matrix differential equation\n$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x(t) + \\begin{bmatrix}-e^{t} \\\\ 3e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhich of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n$x_{1}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}, x_{2}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}, x_{3}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186129596 :question/answer-fields 17592186127027] [17592186171722 :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z) = x-y-z$ on the intersection of the plane $x+y+z=2$ and the cylinder $x^2+z^2=4$."] [17592186171722 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = x-y-z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+z=2, \\qquad h(x,y,z)=x^2 + z^2 = 4\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint.\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{∇f(x, y, z) = λ∇g(x, y, z) + μ∇h(x, y, z); g(x, y, z) = 2; h(x, y, z) = 4\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y, z) &= λ∇g(x, y, z) + μ∇h(x, y, z) \\\\\n⟨f_{x}, f_{y}, f_{z}⟩ &= λ⟨g_{x}, g_{y}, g_{z}⟩ + μ⟨h_{x}, h_{y}, h_{z}⟩ \\\\\n⟨1,-1,-1⟩ &= λ⟨1, 1, 1⟩ + μ⟨2x, 0, 2z⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{1 = λ + 2μx; - 1 = λ; - 1 = λ + 2μz⇒{x = \\frac{1}{μ}; λ =-1; μz = 0\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu$ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction. So, from the third equation above, we must have $z=0$.\n\nNext, we substitute $z=0$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + z^{2} &= 4 \\\\\nx^{2} &= 4 \\\\\nx &= ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=2$ and $z= 0$, we have\n$x + y + z|= 2; 2 + y + 0|= 2; 2 + y|= 2; y|= 0$.\n- If $x=-2$ and $z=0$, we have\n$x + y + z|= 2; - 2 + y + 0|= 2; - 2 + y|= 2; y|= 4$.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n$(2, 0, 0), \\qquad (-2, 4, 0)$.\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(2, 0, 0)$ | $(-2, 4, 0)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $2$ | $-6$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2, 4, 0)$, and its minimum value is $-6$."] [17592186171723 :question/problem "Suppose we want to find the minimum of the function $f(x,y,z)=2xz+y^2$ on the intersection of the surfaces $x-z=2$ and $x^2+y^2+2z^2=3$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ satisfy the following system:\n\n${⟨2z, A A, 2x⟩ = λ⟨1, 0, A A⟩ + μ⟨2x, 2y, A A⟩; x - z = 2; x^{2} + y^{2} + 2z^{2} = 3$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?"] [17592186171723 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = 2xz+y^2\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-z=2, \\qquad h(x,y,z)=x^2+y^2+2z^2=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{∇f(x, y, z) = λ∇g(x, y, z) + μ∇h(x, y, z); g(x, y, z) = 2; h(x, y, z) = 3\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y, z) &= λ∇g(x, y, z) + μ∇h(x, y, z) \\\\\n⟨f_{x}, f_{y}, f_{z}⟩ &= λ⟨g_{x}, g_{y}, g_{z}⟩ + μ⟨h_{x}, h_{y}, h_{z}⟩ \\\\\n⟨2z, 2 y, 2x⟩ &= λ⟨1, 0,-1⟩ + μ⟨2x, 2y, 4 z⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts are $2y,\\:$ $-1,\\:$ and $4z$."] [17592186172391 :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x, y, z) = x + 2y - 6z$ on the intersection of the plane $2x - 3y + 2z = 0$ and the cylinder $x^{2} + y^{2} = 2$."] [17592186172391 :question/answer-fields 17592186169179] [17592186172293 :question/problem "![](images/d3/d33f9b851125443ff738fa4c3aedcbeaf8a202d05caa06747e74262c0ae89bd9.png)\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}ds$, where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r = e^{2θ}$ for $0 \\le θ \\le \\frac{π}{2}$, shown above."] [17592186172293 :question/answer-fields 17592186168591] [17592186120306 :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nAssume that $x_{1}, x_{2}$, and $x_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The equation $x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} + x_{3}a_{3} = 0$ has only the trivial solution $x_{1} = x_{2} = x_{3} = 0$.\n2. The vectors $a_{1}, a_{2}$, and $a_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n3. The vectors $a_{1}, a_{2}$, and $a_{3}$ are linearly independent."] [17592186120306 :question/answer-fields 17592186117945] [17592186120324 :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nAssume that $x_{1}, x_{2}$, and $x_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The equation $x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} + x_{3}a_{3} = 0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n2. The vectors $a_{1}, a_{2}$, and $a_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n3. The vectors $a_{1}, a_{2}$, and $a_{3}$ are linearly independent."] [17592186120324 :question/answer-fields 17592186118053] [17592186120253 :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(-2, 2, 0)$ and is perpendicular to the line\n${x = 3 - 4t; y = 1 + 6t; z =-1 + 2t$."] [17592186120253 :question/answer-fields 17592186117647] [17592186133613 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns $\\mathbf{q}_1,\\mathbf{q}_2$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find the value of $q_{11} \\cdot r_{12}$."] [17592186133613 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2$ be the columns of $A$.\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}\n$$\n\nusing the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{2} &= a_{2} - proj_{v_{1}}a_{2} \\\\\n&= a_{2} - \\frac{a_{2} \\cdot v_{1}}{v_{1} \\cdot v_{1}}v_{1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{12}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} - 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq_{1} &= \\frac{v_{1}}{‖v_{1}‖} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nq_{2} &= \\frac{v_{2}}{‖v_{2}‖} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR &= Q^{T}A \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & 2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nq_{11} \\cdot r_{12} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right) \\cdot \\left({2\\sqrt6}\\right) = 2\n$$"] [17592186133639 :question/problem "$[ \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; a_{1} \\mid a_{2} \\mid a_{3}; \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid ]$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where $\\big\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2,\\mathbf{v}_3 \\big\\}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}_1\\Vert =2$, $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}_2\\Vert =3$, $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}_3\\Vert =4$, and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A$.\n${v_{1} = a_{1}; v_{2} = a_{2} - v_{1}; v_{3} = a_{3} - 3v_{1} + 2v_{2}$.\n\nWhat is the value of $r_{13}+r_{23}+r_{33}$?"] [17592186133639 :question/worked-solution "First, we normalize the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2,\\mathbf{v}_3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq_{1} &= \\frac{v_{1}}{‖v_{1}‖} = \\frac{v_{1}}{2}⟹v_{1} = 2q_{1} \\\\\nq_{2} &= \\frac{v_{2}}{‖v_{2}‖} = \\frac{v_{2}}{3}⟹v_{2} = 3q_{2} \\\\\nq_{3} &= \\frac{v_{3}}{‖v_{3}‖} = \\frac{v_{3}}{4}⟹v_{3} = 4q_{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set $\\big\\{\\mathbf{q}_1,\\mathbf{q}_2,\\mathbf{q}_3 \\big\\}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\mathbf{q}_3$:\n\n$$\n{v_{1} = a_{1}; v_{2} = a_{2} - v_{1}; v_{3} = a_{3} - 3v_{1} + 2v_{2}\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n$[\\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; a_{1} \\mid a_{2} \\mid a_{3}; \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid]$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr_{13}+r_{23}+r_{33} = 6+(-6)+4 = 4\n$$"] [17592186133684 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A$."] [17592186133684 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by $Q^T$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ^{T} \\cdot A &= Q^{T} \\cdot QR \\\\\nQ^{T}A &= (Q^{T}Q)R \\\\\nQ^{T}A &= I_{2}R \\\\\nR &= Q^{T}A\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $Q^TQ = I_2$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR &= Q^{T}A \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133685 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns $\\mathbf{q}_1,\\mathbf{q}_2$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A$, what is the value of $q_{12} \\cdot r_{22}$?"] [17592186133685 :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct a matrix $Q$, we simply normalize $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2\\mathbf{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq_{1} &= \\frac{a_{1}}{‖a_{1}‖} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nq_{2} &= \\frac{a_{2}}{‖a_{2}‖} = \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by $Q^T$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR &= Q^{T}A \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nq_{12} \\cdot r_{22} = -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\cdot 3 = -2\n$$"] [17592186133686 :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $3 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $Q$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix\n2. The columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n3. $R$ is an upper triangular matrix"] [17592186133686 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$, then\n\n$$\n\\underset{{\\large m} \\times {\\large n}}{A} = \\underset{{\\large m} \\times {\\large n}}{Q} \\times \\underset{{\\large n} \\times {\\large n}}{R}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $Q$ is an $m \\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n- $R$ is an $n \\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. $Q$ should be a $3\\times 2$ matrix.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $R$ must be a $2 \\times 2$ upper triangular matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186134332 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(A) = \\begin{bmatrix}q_{11} & q 12 \\\\ q_{21} & q_{22} \\\\ q_{31} & q_{32}\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(Q)\\begin{bmatrix}r_{11} & r 12 \\\\ 0 & r_{22}\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(R)$.\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns $q_{1}, q_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find the value of $q_{12} \\cdot r_{12}$."] [17592186134332 :question/answer-fields 17592186132001] [17592186134554 :question/problem "Given that $A = QR$ is a QR factorization of a $m \\cdot n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $Q$ is a square matrix\n2. $Q$ is an upper triangular matrix\n3. $R$ is an upper triangular matrix"] [17592186134554 :question/answer-fields 17592186133332] [17592186064538 :question/problem "$⌈ - 4⌉ =$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186064538 :question/answer-fields 17592186061398] [17592186152397 :question/problem "What is $lim_(n → ∞)(1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{nx/2}$?"] [17592186152397 :question/answer-fields 17592186148883] [17592186125071 :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x, y) = xy - x$ on the closed triangular region $D$ with vertices $(0, 0), (1, 0), (0, 2)$."] [17592186125071 :question/answer-fields 17592186122996] [17592186088454 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x = \\sqrt{2}\\sec t, y = \\sqrt{6}\\tan t$."] [17592186088454 :question/answer-fields 17592186087347] [17592186133618 :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR$, find the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186133618 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nsatisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{T}Ax &= A^{T}b \\\\\n(QR)^{T}QRx &= (QR)^{T}b \\\\\nR^{T}(Q^{T}Q)Rx &= R^{T}Q^{T}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, it satisfies $Q^T Q = I$, and we get\n\n$$\nR^T R \\mathbf{x} = R^T \\! Q^T \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R^T$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $(R^T)^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(R^{T})^{-1} \\cdot R^{T}Rx &= (R^{T})^{-1} \\cdot R^{T}Q^{T}b \\\\\nRx &= Q^{T}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(R)x &= \\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(Q^{T})\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(b) \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x &= \\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n{7x_{1} - 3x_{2} = 28; 2x_{2} = 14\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=7$. Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x_{1} - 3(7) &= 28 \\\\\n7x_{1} &= 49 \\\\\nx_{1} &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis $\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186133725 :question/problem "$\\bbox[5px, border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}} \\, \\mathbf{x} = \\bbox[5px, border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}} \\, \\mathbf{b}$\n\nLet $A=QR$ be a QR decomposition of an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the matrix equation above gives the least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$. From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?"] [17592186133725 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nsatisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\nA^T A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{T}Ax &= A^{T}b \\\\\n(QR)^{T}QRx &= (QR)^{T}b \\\\\nR^{T}(Q^{T}Q)Rx &= R^{T}Q^{T}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, it satisfies $Q^T Q = I$, and we get\n\n$$\nR^T R \\mathbf{x} = R^T \\! Q^T \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R^T$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $(R^T)^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(R^{T})^{-1} \\cdot R^{T}Rx &= (R^{T})^{-1} \\cdot R^{T}Q^{T}b \\\\\nR \\mid x &= Q Tb\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are $R$ and $Q^T$."] [17592186134116 :question/problem "$RA = Ab$\n\nLet $A = QR$ be a QR decomposition of an $m \\cdot n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the matrix equation above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax = b$. From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?"] [17592186134116 :question/answer-fields 17592186130822] [17592186134341 :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A = QR$, find the least-squares solution to $Ax = b$, where\n$Q = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, R = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186134341 :question/answer-fields 17592186132055] [17592186167093 :question/problem "![](images/bb/bba667daa6bfd40eeb5450cc86066184819b903ace2641bc58c6b5c83869a4b6.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n1. $y^{′} = y^{2} - 2y$\n2. $y^{′} = y^{2} - 4y + 4$\n3. $y^{′} = 2y - y^{2}$"] [17592186167093 :question/answer-fields 17592186163606] [17592186180282 :question/problem "Let $R = [2, 5] \\cdot [0, 2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = 30 - x^{2} - y^{2}$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nGiven that the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n$U(f, P) = ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)AAAAAΔx_{i}Δy_{j}$\nwhere $Δx_{i} = x_{i} - x_{i - 1}$ and $Δy_{j} = y_{j} - y_{j - 1}$, what is the missing expression in the double summation above?"] [17592186180282 :question/answer-fields 17592186179206] [17592186152385 :question/problem "$u→^(A - 2 I)v→^(A - 2 I)w→^(A - 2 I)0; x→^(A + 3 I)y→^(A + 3 I)0$\nGiven the action of the matrices $A - 2I$ and $A + 3I$ on the nonzero vectors $u, v, w, x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $w$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 2$ of $A$\n2. $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 2$ of $A$\n3. $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ =-3$ of $A$"] [17592186152385 :question/answer-fields 17592186148811] [17592186152387 :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}, v = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ =-3$ of $A$\n2. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ =-3$ of $A$\n3. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ =-3$ of $A$"] [17592186152387 :question/answer-fields 17592186148823] [17592186228925 :question/problem "A supply crate of mass $24\\,\\textrm{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the crate's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\geq 0$ seconds after the descent began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the crate is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=\\dfrac{4}{5}\\,\\textrm{kg/m}$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nWhat differential equation best models the velocity? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g$, and the only forces acting on the crate are the weight due to gravity and drag."] [17592186228925 :question/worked-solution "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a{:}$\n$F = ma$\n\nIn our case, two forces are acting on the crate:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the crate, which equals\n$w|= mg; = 24g$.\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D$, which is proportional to the square of the velocity of the crate (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n$D = -kv^2 = -\\dfrac{4}{5}v^2$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the crate is\n\n$$\nF = w + D = 24g - \\dfrac{4}{5}v^2\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity:\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the crate, we have\n\n$$\n24g - \\dfrac{4}{5}v^2 =24\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} = g - \\dfrac{1}{30}v^2\n$$"] [17592186228926 :question/problem "A concrete cylinder of mass $50\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is released vertically downward with an initial velocity of $9\\,\\textrm{m/s}$. Let $v(t)$ be the cylinder's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\geq 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the cylinder is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=4.9\\,\\textrm{kg/m}$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t)$. Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$, and the only forces acting on the cylinder are the weight due to gravity and drag."] [17592186228926 :question/worked-solution "First, note that only two forces act on the package: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw = mg = 50g\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D$, which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD = -kv^2 = -4.9v^2\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the package is\n\n$$\nF = w + D =50g - 4.9v^2\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= ma \\\\\n50g - 4.9v^{2} &= 50\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n\\frac{dv}{dt} &= g - 0.098v^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dv}{dt} &= g(1 - 0.01v^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{1 - 0.01v^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} &= g \\\\\n\\int\\frac{1}{1 - 0.01v^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}dt &= g\\intdt \\\\\n\\int(1)/(1 - (0.1v)^{2})dv &= g\\intdt \\\\\n\\frac{1}{0.1}artanh(0.1v) &= gt + C_{1} \\\\\nartanh(0.1v) &= \\frac{1}{10}gt + C \\\\\n0.1v &= \\tanh (\\frac{1}{10}gt + C) \\\\\nv(t) &= 10\\tanh (\\frac{1}{10}gt + C)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = 0.1C_1$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the package is released with an initial velocity of $9\\,\\textrm{m/s}$. So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0) = 9$, and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(0) &= 10\\tanh (\\frac{1}{10}g \\cdot 0 + C) \\\\\n9 &= 10\\tanh (C) \\\\\n\\tanh (C) &= 0.9 \\\\\nC &= artanh(0.9) \\approx 1.472\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\nv(t) = 10\\tanh\\left(\\dfrac1{10}g t+1.472\\right)\n$$"] [17592186228927 :question/problem "A body of mass $80\\,\\textrm{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the body is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=1\\,\\textrm{kg/m}$ and acts opposite to the direction of motion.\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity $v_\\text{term}$ of the body? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$, and the only forces acting on the body are the weight due to gravity and drag."] [17592186228927 :question/worked-solution "Terminal velocity is the constant speed a falling object approaches as the upward resistive force (drag) grows to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\nThat is, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma$, the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the body: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw = mg = 80 \\cdot 9.8 = 784\\,\\textrm{N}\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D$, which is proportional to the square of the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD = -kv^2 = -v^2\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF = w + D =784 - v^2\n$$\n\nWhen the body reaches terminal velocity $v_\\text{term}$, the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n784 - (v_\\text{term})^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad v_\\text{term} = \\sqrt{784} =28\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the body is\n\n$$\nv_\\text{term} =28\\,\\textrm{m/s}\n$$"] [17592186228929 :question/problem "An air mattress of mass $5\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is released from rest and falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the mattress's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\geq 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the air mattress is proportional to the square of its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the air mattress’s velocity is $0.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $7.35\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t)$.\nAssume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$, and the only forces acting on the air mattress are the weight due to gravity and drag."] [17592186228929 :question/worked-solution "First, note that only two forces act on the air mattress: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n$w = mg = 5g$\nand the force due to drag $D$, which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n$D=-kv^2$,\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the air mattress is\n\n$$\nF = w + D = 5g - kv^2\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= ma \\\\\n5g - kv^{2} &= 5\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n\\frac{dv}{dt} &= g - \\frac{k}{5}v^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the air mattress's velocity is $0.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $7.35\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$. Substituting these values and $g = 9.8$ into the ODE, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n7.35 = g - \\dfrac{k}{5} \\cdot (0.5)^2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k = 49\\,\\textrm{kg/m}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} = g - 9.8v^2\n$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t$, we get\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the air mattress is released from rest. So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0) = 0$, and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(0) &= \\tanh (g \\cdot 0 + C) \\\\\n0 &= \\tanh (C) \\\\\nC &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\nv(t) = \\tanh\\left(gt\\right)\n$$"] [17592186229674 :question/problem "A body of mass $25kg$ is released from rest and falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the body's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the body is proportional to the square of its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the body’s velocity is $1.25m/s$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $7.35m/s^{2}$.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t)$.\nAssume the acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8m/s^{2}$, and the only forces acting on the body are the weight due to gravity and drag."] [17592186229674 :question/answer-fields 17592186228208] [17592186078977 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^{11}f(x)dx = 13$ and $\\displaystyle \\int_{15}^{11}7f(x)dx = 21$. What is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^{15}f(x)dx$?"] [17592186078977 :question/answer-fields 17592186076610] [17592186157975 :question/problem "![](images/54/54e53a7856a2266ece211575a240b4cc7759511ae2ff70cfb8a8a870912ecb4a.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y = ± \\frac{3}{4}x$."] [17592186157975 :question/answer-fields 17592186156741] [17592186133707 :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ for every matrix $A$?\n\n1. $\\text{Null}(A)$\n2. $\\text{Col}(A)$\n3. $\\text{Null}(A^T)$"] [17592186133707 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ is the row space of $A$, and consists of all linear combinations of the rows of $A$.\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of $A$, it must be orthogonal to each row of $A$.\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by $A$, we multiply each row of $A$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ must be orthogonal to the null space of $A$, which is $\\text{Null}(A)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)"] [17592186133709 :question/problem "Consider a $8 \\times 6$ matrix $A$. If $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) = 3$, then find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A^T))$."] [17592186133709 :question/worked-solution "The four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/889b83fadd26b377c4dd34143c9b16541630c04e90de3d9b005e7147ead75ad5.png)\n\nIn our case, we are given that $m=8$, $n=6$, and $n-r=3$.\n\nSubstituting $n=6$ into $n-r=3$, we can find the rank of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n6 - r = 3 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad r = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndim(Col(A^{T})) &= r = 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133710 :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $m \\times n$ matrix. What is the image of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ under the action of $A$?"] [17592186133710 :question/worked-solution "Pre-multiplying a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ by $A$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n[ \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid ⋯ \\mid \\mid; a_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}a_{2} & ⋯\\end{vmatrix}a_{n}; \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid ⋯ \\mid \\mid ]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ under the action of $A$ must be $\\text{Col}(A)$, the column space of $A$.\n\nFurthermore, since $\\text{Null}(A)$ is mapped to the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ by the definition of the null subspace, the image of its orthogonal complement $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ must be $\\text{Col}(A)$.\n\nThis can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8b/8b08bdf79d53f312cabd665bcd4669795bd8851dfb33d5e5db6178bd80b24398.png)"] [17592186134076 :question/problem "Consider a $3 \\cdot 4$ matrix $A$. If $dim(Null(A^{T})) = 2$, then $dim(Col(A)) =$"] [17592186134076 :question/answer-fields 17592186130582] [17592186134077 :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $Null(A)$ for every matrix $A$?\n\n1. $Null(A^{T})$\n2. $Col(A^{T})$\n3. $Col(A)$"] [17592186134077 :question/answer-fields 17592186130588] [17592186134082 :question/problem "Consider a $7 \\cdot 5$ matrix $A$. If $dim(Col(A)) = 4$, then $dim(Null(A^{T})) =$"] [17592186134082 :question/answer-fields 17592186130618] [17592186134083 :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $Null(XY)$ for all compatible matrices $X$ and $Y$?\n\n1. $Null(Y^{T}X^{T})$\n2. $Col(XY)$\n3. $Col(Y^{T}X^{T})$"] [17592186134083 :question/answer-fields 17592186130624] [17592186228943 :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y'' + 16y = \\sin 4x, \\qquad y(0) = 0, \\quad y(\\pi) = -\\dfrac\\pi 8,\\quad 0\\leq x \\leq \\pi$\n\nThe solution is given by\n\n$y = f(x) + B \\cdot g(x)$\nwhere $B\\in \\mathbb R$ is arbitrary. Find the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$."] [17592186228943 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP)\n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\nwhere $P, Q$, and $f$ are continuous on $[a,b]$, and $\\alpha_i$ and $\\beta_i$ are real constants such that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0)\n$$\n\nIf the associated homogeneous BVP has nontrivial solutions, then the inhomogeneous BVP has either no solution or infinitely many solutions.\n\nTo find the solution of an inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP):\n\n- First, we find the general solution $y_h(x)$ of the corresponding homogeneous equation.\n- Then, we find a particular solution $y_p(x)$ of the inhomogeneous equation.\n- Finally, we apply the boundary conditions to determine the arbitrary constants.\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set\n\n$$\ny = e^{m x}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^{2} + 16 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nm = \\pm 4\\textrm i\n$$\n\nSo, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos 4x, \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin 4x\n$$\n\nand the general solution to the homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\ny_h = A\\cos 4x + B\\sin 4x\n$$\n\nNext, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = Cx\\cos 4x + Dx\\sin 4x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′}(x) &= C\\cos 4x - 4Cx\\sin 4x + D\\sin 4x + 4Dx\\cos 4x \\\\\ny_{p}^{″}(x) &= -8C\\sin 4x - 16Cx\\cos 4x + 8D\\cos 4x - 16Dx\\sin 4x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we reach (after some cancellation)\n\n$$\n-8C\\sin 4x + 8D\\cos 4x = \\sin 4x\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nC = -\\dfrac18\n$$\n\nand $D = 0$. Therefore, our general solution is\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos 4x + B\\sin 4x -\\dfrac18x\\cos4x\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(0) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\nA\\cos (4\\cdot 0) + B\\sin (4\\cdot 0) - \\dfrac18 \\cdot 0 \\cdot \\cos (4\\cdot 0) = 0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A = 0$. So, we have\n\n$$\ny = B\\sin 4x -\\dfrac18x\\cos 4x\n$$\n\nApplying the condition\n\n$$\ny(\\pi) = -\\dfrac\\pi8\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\nB\\sin (4π) - \\frac{π}{8}\\cos (4π) =-\\frac{π}{8}; 0 - \\frac{π}{8} =-\\frac{π}{8}\n$$\n\nwhich is true for all values of $B$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the boundary value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= -\\frac{1}{8}x\\cos 4x + B \\cdot \\sin 4x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is arbitrary.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(x) = -\\dfrac18 x\\cos 4x, \\qquad g(x) = \\sin 4x\n$$\n\n**Note**: The associated homogeneous BVP\n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = 0, \\qquad y(0) = 0, \\quad y(\\pi) = 0,\\quad 0\\leq x \\leq \\pi\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions. Therefore, the inhomogeneous BVP has either infinitely many solutions or no solution. Since we have found a solution that contains an arbitrary constant and satisfies both boundary conditions, the BVP has infinitely many solutions."] [17592186228944 :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y'' - 9y = -9x - 3, \\qquad y(1) = \\dfrac{4}{3}, \\quad y(2) = \\dfrac{13}{3},\\quad 1\\leq x \\leq 2$\n\nThe solution is given by\n\n$y = Ae^{3x} + Be^{-3x} + g(x)$.\nFind the constants $A$ and $B$, and the function $g(x)$."] [17592186228944 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP)\n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x), \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = \\gamma_1, \\qquad \\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = \\gamma_2\n$$\n\nwhere $P, Q$, and $f$ are continuous on $[a,b]$, and $\\alpha_i$ and $\\beta_i$ are real constants such that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0)\n$$\n\nThe inhomogeneous BVP has at most one solution if and only if the associated homogeneous BVP has only the trivial solution $y \\equiv 0$.\n\nTo find the solution of an inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP):\n\n- First, we find the general solution $y_h(x)$ of the corresponding homogeneous equation.\n- Then, we find a particular solution $y_p(x)$ of the inhomogeneous equation.\n- Finally, we apply the boundary conditions to determine the arbitrary constants.\n\nThe homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\ny'' - 9y = 0\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set\n\n$$\ny = e^{m x}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^{2} - 9 &= 0 \\\\\n(m - 3)(m + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m = 3$ and $m = -3$. So, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{3x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = e^{-3x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\ny_h = Ae^{3x} + Be^{-3x}\n$$\n\nNow, we seek a particular solution $y_p$ of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = Cx + D\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our inhomogeneous equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(Cx + D)^{″} - 9(Cx + D) &= -9x - 3 \\\\\n- 9(Cx + D) &= -9x - 3 \\\\\n- 9Cx - 9D &= -9x - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $x$ and constants, respectively, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-9C =-9 & ⇒C = 1 \\\\ -9D =-3 & ⇒D = \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = x + \\dfrac13\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= y_{h} + y_{p} \\\\\n&= Ae^{3x} + Be^{-3x} + x + \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the condition\n\n$$\ny(1) = \\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\nAe^3 + Be^{-3} +\\dfrac43 = \\dfrac{4}{3} \\qquad \\Rightarrow\\qquad B =-Ae^6\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{3x} - Ae^{-3x+6} + x +\\dfrac13\n$$\n\nApplying the condition\n\n$$\ny(2) = \\dfrac{13}{3}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\nAe^6 - Ae^{0} +2 + \\dfrac13 = \\dfrac{13}{3} \\qquad \\Rightarrow\\qquad Ae^6 - A = 2\n$$\n\nSolving for $A$, we have\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{2}{e^6-1}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the first equation and solving for $B$, we have\n\n$$\nB =-Ae^6 = -\\dfrac{2e^6}{e^6-1}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the function $g(x)$, we have\n\n$$\ng(x) = y_p(x) = x + \\dfrac13\n$$\n\n**Note**: The associated homogeneous BVP\n\n$$\ny'' - 9y = 0, \\qquad y(1) = 0, \\quad y(2) = 0,\\quad 1\\leq x \\leq 2\n$$\n\nhas only the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0$. Therefore, the inhomogeneous BVP has at most one solution. Since we have found a solution satisfying both boundary conditions, the solution to this inhomogeneous BVP is unique."] [17592186229732 :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y^{″} = 2x, y(0) = 0, y(1) - y^{′}(1) =-\\frac{2}{3}, 0 \\le x \\le 1$\n\nThe general solution can be written as\n\n$y = f(x) + A \\cdot g(x)$\nwhere $A ∈ R$ is arbitrary.\n\nSelect functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ that make this statement true.\n\n$f(x) =$\n{{field-1}}\n$g(x) =$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186229732 :question/answer-fields 17592186228583] [17592186229732 :question/answer-fields 17592186228579]]